## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cross-account tenant isolation, serverless data flow, and incident response in a real-world AWS stack.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC with cross-account trust; per-tenant prefixes; pre-signed URLs; KMS rotation; logging; exfiltration controls; breach containment orchestration (Step Functions/EventBridge); multi-region auditing.

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-prefixes/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant}"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate that breach containment completes within 60 seconds? 
- What metrics and alarms would you surface to detect access drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests tenant isolation, cross‑account access, and rapid containment in a simple, reproducible pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Cross‑account IAM roles with restricted trust
- S3 per‑tenant prefixes and least privilege in bucket policies
- Server‑side encryption (SSE‑KMS) and key rotation
- Immutable auditability and rapid containment of breaches

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example trust policy snippet (Account B role trusting Account A data role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A:role/DataReaderForTenant*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "tenant-<id>-read"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Example bucket policy restricting to tenant prefix
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B:role/AnalyticsRead"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data-bucket/tenant-<id>/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate keys per tenant and handle revocation?
- How would you automate breach simulations and containment checks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-time detection and automated containment of credential abuse in a multi-account CI/CD setup using OIDC tokens.

## Key Concepts
- OIDC-based cross-account access and trust policies
- Real-time threat detection: CloudTrail, Security Hub, IAM Access Analyzer
- Containment automation: credential rotation, trust policy adjustments, resource quarantine
- Automation tooling: EventBridge, Step Functions, Secrets Manager

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: IAM trust policy snippet with OIDC
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:org/repo:ref:refs/heads/main"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the containment playbook?
- Which logs and metrics confirm rollback reliability and minimal pipeline disruption?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement a robust artifact provenance control across accounts, combining SBOM, signing, trust stores, and ABAC with per-tenant policy, plus emergency containment and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- SBOM, artifact signing (AWS Signer), ECR signing, Lambda layer/version pinning
- Centralized trust store, ABAC with conditions aws:SourceAccount/aws:PrincipalOrgID
- Immutable CloudTrail logs, rapid containment workflow, rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: verify SBOM, check signer, enforce policy before deploy
async function gateArtifact(a) {
  const sbom = getSBOM(a);
  if(!verifySBOM(sbom)) throw 'SBOM invalid';
  if(!isSigned(a)) throw 'Not signed';
  if(!policyAllows(a)) throw 'Access denied';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the containment workflow? 
- How to handle compromised signing keys and key rotation?

